# PROJECT: FINDORA — INTELLIGENT DIGITAL LOST & FOUND

Act as a world-class product designer, senior full-stack engineer, UI/UX specialist, database architect, AI engineer, cybersecurity engineer, and technical product manager.

Your mission is to build, improve, and deliver **Findora**, a highly polished, fully functional, intelligent Digital Lost & Found platform for colleges, universities, offices, residential communities, and other shared environments.

This project addresses PS 02 — DIGITAL LOST & FOUND.

Do not create an ordinary listing website. Create a premium, intelligent platform that connects people who have lost belongings with people who have found them, helps verify ownership, and enables secure item recovery.

The product's central promise is:

"Don't scroll a list. Get matched with what you lost."

The application must combine an outstanding frontend, intuitive user experience, interactive maps, intelligent matching, identity verification, privacy protection, secure claims, real-time communication, and reliable backend functionality.

## CRITICAL IMPLEMENTATION RULES

1. If an existing project or codebase is supplied, inspect its structure, dependencies, database configuration, authentication system, and existing features before making changes.
2. Preserve working functionality and improve the existing application instead of unnecessarily rebuilding everything.
3. Do not remove existing user data, credentials, environment configuration, or functional integrations.
4. Implement real application functionality wherever the requirements call for it.
5. Do not create decorative buttons that do nothing.
6. Do not simulate successful authentication, verification, database writes, payments, notifications, or item returns.
7. Never expose API keys, private credentials, service-role keys, or sensitive verification data in frontend code.
8. Use realistic sample data only in a clearly identified development or demonstration environment. The production application must use real persisted data.
9. If an external service requires credentials, provide clear setup instructions and environment-variable names. Do not pretend that an unconfigured integration is working.
10. Do not leave core functionality as TODO comments, placeholder pages, or disconnected mock interfaces.
11. Test the application after implementation, resolve errors, and verify the complete user journey.
12. If a requirement cannot be completed because an external credential or service is unavailable, implement the integration interface and report precisely what configuration is still required.

Prioritize a coherent, working application over adding an excessive number of unfinished features.

